@startuml
title Slurm + Dask Deployment (single sbatch allocation)

' Compact layout: edge labels are hand-wrapped and the driver note is anchored
' sideways so the figure stays close to square -- see README, 'Layout'.
skinparam componentStyle rectangle
skinparam nodesep 15
skinparam ranksep 30

actor "Cluster scheduler\n(slurmctld / slurmd)" as Slurm

node "Slurm allocation\n(examples/hpc/slurm/train_dask.sbatch)" {
  node "Batch step (node 0)" as Batch {
    component "Driver process\n(examples/hpc/train_ea_dask.py,\nor any make_executor caller)" as Driver
  }

  node "srun --overlap step 1 (node 0)" as SchedStep {
    component "dask scheduler\n--scheduler-file $SCHED_FILE" as Scheduler
  }

  node "srun --overlap step 2\n(--ntasks=$SLURM_JOB_NUM_NODES)" as WorkStep {
    component "dask worker(s), one task per node\n--nthreads 1\n--nworkers $SLURM_CPUS_PER_TASK\n(node 0's task shares the node\nwith the scheduler and driver)" as W
  }
}

database "Shared filesystem\n($SHARED_DIR)" as FS {
  artifact "$SCHED_FILE\n(connection info)" as SchedFile
  artifact "TrainingConfig\n.output_dir" as Results
}

Slurm -> Batch : allocates nodes,\nlaunches sbatch
Batch -> SchedStep : srun --overlap\n(shares CPUs)
Batch -> WorkStep : srun --overlap\n(shares CPUs)

Scheduler -> SchedFile : writes address\n+ ports on bind
Driver -> SchedFile : polls up to 60s,\nthen reads
W --> SchedFile : reads to locate\nscheduler

Driver ..> Scheduler : DEAP_DASK_SCHEDULER holds\n$SCHED_FILE -> make_executor\n-> resolve_scheduler_address\n-> Client(scheduler_file=...)
W ..> Scheduler : registers\nas worker

Driver -> Scheduler : client.map(\n_worker_evaluate,\npayloads)
Scheduler --> W : task
W --> Scheduler : (fitness,\nsteps_delta)
Scheduler --> Driver : gathered results\n(ordered; retries=2\non worker death)

Driver -> Results : metrics.csv,\nsummary.json,\ncheckpoint -- sole\nwriter (ExperimentLogger)

note left of Driver
  Real-cluster deltas vs the
  Docker Slurm rig (docker/slurm-cluster/,
  tests/hpc/test_slurm_rig.py) are only
  partition/account sbatch headers and
  node counts -- the same .sbatch file
  is submitted in both.
end note

note bottom of FS
  The scheduler-file handshake is the
  canonical dask-on-Slurm pattern: the
  scheduler's host/port are unknown
  before the allocation, so driver and
  workers discover them through a
  shared-FS file rather than a fixed
  tcp:// address (which the Docker Dask
  rig, docker/dask-cluster/, uses).
end note
@enduml
